# THE GARDEN OF EDEN and the HEDLUND characterization of a cellular automaton, modeled and proved by the kernel. A
# cellular automaton is a grid of cells, each holding a state, all updating in lockstep by one fixed LOCAL RULE that reads
# a cell and its neighbors. The global map carries a whole configuration to the next one. A GARDEN OF EDEN is a
# configuration with NO PREDECESSOR: a pattern that can be a starting state but can never be produced by the rule from any
# earlier state. Gardens of Eden exist exactly when the global map is NOT SURJECTIVE, when some configuration sits outside
# the image of the rule and so is unreachable.
#
# The CURTIS-HEDLUND-LYNDON theorem (Hedlund's theorem) says what a cellular automaton IS in the abstract: precisely a map
# on configuration space that is continuous and COMMUTES WITH THE SHIFT. Shift commutation is the content captured here, it
# is the statement that the rule is the same everywhere, applied uniformly at every cell, so translating the configuration
# and then stepping equals stepping and then translating.
#
# The configuration window here is THREE binary cells arranged in a cyclic row, eight strip states. The shift is the cyclic
# rotation of the three cells. Two rules are studied on this window. The first is a non-surjective local rule (the next
# cell is the conjunction of itself and its right neighbor), applied uniformly with cyclic neighborhoods; it commutes with
# the shift, yet it misses some strips, so a Garden of Eden exists and is exhibited concretely. The second is the cyclic
# rotation itself, a bijection of the eight strips; it commutes with the shift and, being surjective, admits NO Garden of
# Eden, tying reversibility to the absence of unreachable configurations.
load @term/seed/atom/flag
  find flag
  find either

# A cell, the smallest piece of the lattice: off or on, an absent or present mark.
form cell
  case off
  case on

# A strip, the finite configuration window the global map acts on: three cells in a cyclic row. With two states per cell
# there are eight strips. The row is cyclic, so the right neighbor of the third cell is the first cell, which is what lets
# the shift act and the local rule apply uniformly at every position.
form strip
  case row
    link one, like cell
    link two, like cell
    link three, like cell

# THE LOCAL RULE, the lookup table at one cell: the next state of a cell is the conjunction of the cell and its right
# neighbor, on exactly when both are on. This is a fixed two-input rule, read off a neighborhood and applied identically
# everywhere. Conjunction collapses information (it forgets the off-off, off-on, on-off cases into off), which is why the
# global map it drives is not surjective.
task local-rule
  take here, like cell
  take right, like cell
  like cell
  fork case, read here
    case off
      send back
        make off
    case on
      fork case, read right
        case off
          send back
            make off
        case on
          send back
            make on

# THE GLOBAL STEP, the local rule applied uniformly across the cyclic strip: each cell becomes the conjunction of itself
# and its right neighbor, with the third cell wrapping to the first. This is the global map on the finite window, the same
# rule at every position. Because conjunction is many-to-one, this map is not onto the eight strips.
task step
  take s, like strip
  like strip
  fork case, read s
    case row
      link one
      link two
      link three
      send back
        make row
          bind one
            call local-rule
              read one
              read two
          bind two
            call local-rule
              read two
              read three
          bind three
            call local-rule
              read three
              read one

# THE SHIFT, the cyclic rotation of the strip: each cell moves one position to the left, the first cell wrapping to the
# end. The strip (a, b, c) becomes (b, c, a). This is the hyperbolic translation of the line made finite, the symmetry the
# Hedlund theorem says a cellular automaton must commute with.
task shift
  take s, like strip
  like strip
  fork case, read s
    case row
      link one
      link two
      link three
      send back
        make row
          bind one
            read two
          bind two
            read three
          bind three
            read one

# TEMPLATE: one strip literal built from three cell states, used to enumerate the eight inputs of the predecessor search
# and to exhibit concrete configurations.
tree strip-of
  take a
  take b
  take c

  hook fuse
    make row
      bind one
        make {a}
      bind two
        make {b}
      bind three
        make {c}

# The three projections of a strip, reading one cell position each. They let the equality test compare two strips position
# by position without destructuring both at once.
task cell-one
  take s, like strip
  like cell
  fork case, read s
    case row
      link one
      link two
      link three
      send back
        read one

task cell-two
  take s, like strip
  like cell
  fork case, read s
    case row
      link one
      link two
      link three
      send back
        read two

task cell-three
  take s, like strip
  like cell
  fork case, read s
    case row
      link one
      link two
      link three
      send back
        read three

# Strip equality: two strips are equal when their three cells agree. The predecessor search compares a candidate image
# against a target through this test.
task is-same
  take x, like strip
  take y, like strip
  like flag
  send back
    call both
      call both
        call cell-same
          call cell-one
            read x
          call cell-one
            read y
        call cell-same
          call cell-two
            read x
          call cell-two
            read y
      call cell-same
        call cell-three
          read x
        call cell-three
          read y

# Cell equality, the per-position test the strip comparison is built from.
task cell-same
  take p, like cell
  take q, like cell
  like flag
  fork case, read p
    case off
      fork case, read q
        case off
          send back
            make yes
        case on
          send back
            make no
    case on
      fork case, read q
        case off
          send back
            make no
        case on
          send back
            make yes

# WHETHER A STRIP HAS A PREDECESSOR under the conjunction step: yes when SOME strip steps to the target, found by searching
# all eight inputs. The search is a finite disjunction over every strip, comparing its image to the target. This is the
# computable test for reachability, and a Garden of Eden is exactly a strip on which it returns no.
task has-predecessor
  take target, like strip
  like flag
  send back
    call either
      call either
        call either
          call image-hits
            fuse strip-of, off, off, off
            read target
          call image-hits
            fuse strip-of, off, off, on
            read target
        call either
          call image-hits
            fuse strip-of, off, on, off
            read target
          call image-hits
            fuse strip-of, off, on, on
            read target
      call either
        call either
          call image-hits
            fuse strip-of, on, off, off
            read target
          call image-hits
            fuse strip-of, on, off, on
            read target
        call either
          call image-hits
            fuse strip-of, on, on, off
            read target
          call image-hits
            fuse strip-of, on, on, on
            read target

# Whether the step of a candidate strip lands on the target: one term of the predecessor disjunction.
task image-hits
  take candidate, like strip
  take target, like strip
  like flag
  send back
    call is-same
      call step
        read candidate
      read target

# THE RULE COMMUTES WITH THE SHIFT, the Hedlund property: step (shift s) = shift (step s), for every strip. The conjunction
# rule is the same at every cell, so translating the configuration and then stepping equals stepping and then translating.
# Proved by exhaustive induction over the eight strips, each resolving by computation. This is the content of the
# Curtis-Hedlund-Lyndon characterization carried here, the statement that the rule is uniform across the lattice.
rule step-commutes-with-shift
  mark s, like strip
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call step
        call shift
          read s
      call shift
        call step
          read s
  fold s

# A GARDEN OF EDEN EXISTS: the strip (on, on, off) has NO PREDECESSOR under the conjunction step. The image of the step over
# all eight inputs is the five strips off-off-off, off-on-off, off-off-on, on-off-off, and on-on-on; the strip on-on-off is
# not among them, so no strip steps to it. has-predecessor of this strip is no, witnessed by the search across the whole
# input space. This is a concrete unreachable configuration, the proof that the conjunction global map is not surjective.
rule garden-of-eden-has-no-predecessor
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call has-predecessor
        fuse strip-of, on, on, off
      make no
  calm hold

# A REACHABLE CONFIGURATION HAS A PREDECESSOR: the all-off strip is the image of itself (and of others) under the
# conjunction step, so has-predecessor returns yes. This shows the predecessor test is non-trivial, separating the
# reachable strips from the Gardens of Eden rather than rejecting everything.
rule reachable-strip-has-a-predecessor
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call has-predecessor
        fuse strip-of, off, off, off
      make yes
  calm hold

# THE SHIFT ITSELF IS A REVERSIBLE RULE that commutes with the shift: a cellular automaton whose global map is the cyclic
# rotation. shift (shift s) over the three-cell strip composed three times returns the original, so it is a bijection of the
# eight strips. As a translation it trivially commutes with the shift, satisfying the Hedlund property. Being a bijection it
# is surjective, so under this rule EVERY configuration has a predecessor and there is NO Garden of Eden. The two laws below
# witness that absence.
#
# WHETHER A STRIP HAS A PREDECESSOR under the rotation rule: the same finite search, now stepping each candidate by the
# shift. Because the shift is a bijection every target is hit, so this returns yes on every strip.
task has-predecessor-by-shift
  take target, like strip
  like flag
  send back
    call either
      call either
        call either
          call shift-hits
            fuse strip-of, off, off, off
            read target
          call shift-hits
            fuse strip-of, off, off, on
            read target
        call either
          call shift-hits
            fuse strip-of, off, on, off
            read target
          call shift-hits
            fuse strip-of, off, on, on
            read target
      call either
        call either
          call shift-hits
            fuse strip-of, on, off, off
            read target
          call shift-hits
            fuse strip-of, on, off, on
            read target
        call either
          call shift-hits
            fuse strip-of, on, on, off
            read target
          call shift-hits
            fuse strip-of, on, on, on
            read target

# Whether the shift of a candidate strip lands on the target: one term of the rotation predecessor disjunction.
task shift-hits
  take candidate, like strip
  take target, like strip
  like flag
  send back
    call is-same
      call shift
        read candidate
      read target

# THE REVERSIBLE RULE HAS NO GARDEN OF EDEN: every strip has a predecessor under the rotation, has-predecessor-by-shift s =
# yes for every strip. The cyclic rotation is a bijection, so its global map is surjective and no configuration is
# unreachable. Proved by exhaustive induction over the eight strips, each finding its preimage by computation. This ties
# reversibility to the absence of Gardens of Eden: an invertible rule loses no configurations, so none are left orphaned.
rule reversible-rule-has-no-garden-of-eden
  mark a, like cell
  mark b, like cell
  mark c, like cell
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call has-predecessor-by-shift
        make row
          bind one
            read a
          bind two
            read b
          bind three
            read c
      make yes
  fold a, b, c

# THE ROTATION IS A GENUINE PERMUTATION, not the identity: the shift maps (off, off, on) to (off, on, off), so the middle
# cell goes from off to on. Reading the middle cell after the shift gives on, not off, two distinct cell states, so the
# rotation cannot be the identity and truly moves the strips. With the no-Garden-of-Eden law above this confirms the
# rotation is a bijection of the eight strips, the reversible counterpart to the collapsing conjunction rule.
rule shift-is-not-identity
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call cell-two
        call shift
          fuse strip-of, off, off, on
      make off
  calm miss
